[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability from the user’s host (host_1_1_1_1) to the server VLAN (10.200.0.0/24) and the core. The host can ping all servers (10.200.0.3–.6) with 0% loss and low RTT, and can reach the DNS server (10.200.0.2). HTTP from host_1_1_1_1 to a server (10.200.0.3) succeeds repeatedly with total times ~2.5–3.2 ms. On the routing side, all three core routers have OSPF-learned routes to 10.200.0.0/24 and the user/other access subnets with correct next-hops, indicating the control plane and core fabric are healthy.

Mechanism-wise, if a core device or OSPF were down, we would see missing OSPF routes and/or loss/blackholing; instead, routing tables and probes show consistent forwarding. Localization therefore does not point to any single device or interface; the user path (host_1_1_1_1 -> access -> dist -> core -> server access) is operational.

Root cause: No persistent fault is present; the reported timeouts cannot be reproduced and there is no evidence of a current core failure.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Advise the user to retest; if issues reappear, capture timestamp and destination IP so we can correlate with routing/CPU logs. As a preventive measure, enable transient event logging/alerts on OSPF adjacencies and server-access links to catch any brief post-maintenance reconvergence in the future.
=== END ===